<branch id="A" type="Concrete">
    <metaphor>A rolling wheel. The circle is a wheel with a marked point. As it rolls one full revolution without slipping, the marked point traces a straight line on the ground. The length of this line is the circumference. The animation shows the wheel rolling, and the traced line is then compared to three diameters laid end-to-end plus a small extra segment, visually revealing Pi.</metaphor>
    <scores pedagogy="9" feasibility="7" engagement="8" />
  </branch>
  <branch id="B" type="Abstract">
    <metaphor>A geometric unrolling. The circle is static. Its circumference is "cut" at a point and "unwrapped" or "flattened" into a straight line segment. This line segment is then measured by aligning multiple copies of the diameter (D) along it. We see that 3 diameters fit, with a leftover piece. Measuring the leftover shows it's about 0.14D, establishing π ≈ 3.14.</metaphor>
    <scores pedagogy="10" feasibility="9" engagement="8" />
  </branch>
  <branch id="C" type="Interactive/Progressive">
    <metaphor>A dynamic morph. The circle deforms continuously: first, it's cut and straightened into a wavy line, which then smooths into a straight segment. This segment is then placed above a number line marked in units of D. The segment's endpoint clearly lands between the 3 and 4 marks. A measurement bar appears, showing the exact ratio C/D = π.</metaphor>
    <scores pedagogy="8" feasibility="6" engagement="9" />
  </branch>
  <selection>
